        "before": "The proposition assumes that \\(X\\) is a smooth affine Hamiltonian \\(G\\)-variety, that \\(Z\\subset\\mu(X)\\) is a locally closed \\(G\\)-stable subset, and that a character \\(\\bar\\lambda\\in\\mathfrak b^*\\) has \\(Z_{\\bar\\lambda}=Z\\cap(\\bar\\lambda+\\mathfrak b^\\perp)\\ne\\varnothing\\). It then declares the following equivalent: \\[\\begin{aligned}\n &\\mu^{-1}(Z_{\\bar\\lambda})\\text{ is Lagrangian in }X; \\tag{a}\\\\\n &\\mu^{-1}(\\lambda)\\text{ is isotropic for every }\\lambda\\in Z_{\\bar\\lambda}; \\tag{b}\\\\\n &\\dim F\\leq \\tfrac12\\bigl(\\dim X-\\dim(G\\lambda)\\bigr)\n \\quad\\text{for every irreducible fiber component }F. \\tag{c}\\end{aligned}\\] There is no openness hypothesis on \\(Z\\) in part (1).",
        "after": "The proposition assumes that \\(X\\) is a smooth affine Hamiltonian \\(G\\)-variety, that \\(Z\\subset\\mu(X)\\) is a locally closed \\(G\\)-stable subset, and that a character \\(\\bar\\lambda\\in\\mathfrak b^*\\) has \\(Z_{\\bar\\lambda}=Z\\cap(\\bar\\lambda+\\mathfrak b^\\perp)\\ne\\varnothing\\). It then declares the following equivalent: \\[\\begin{aligned}\n &\\mu^{-1}(Z_{\\bar\\lambda})\\text{ is Lagrangian in }X; \\qquad\\text{(a)}\\\\\n &\\mu^{-1}(\\lambda)\\text{ is isotropic for every }\\lambda\\in Z_{\\bar\\lambda}; \\qquad\\text{(b)}\\\\\n &\\dim F\\leq \\tfrac12\\bigl(\\dim X-\\dim(G\\lambda)\\bigr)\n \\quad\\text{for every irreducible fiber component }F. \\qquad\\text{(c)}\\end{aligned}\\] There is no openness hypothesis on \\(Z\\) in part (1).",
